Falsifies `machine_discovery::acpi::verification::an_rsdp_is_accepted_only_when_its_bytes_sum_to_zero`.

The defect: the 20-byte checksum is not checked at all, so a revision-0 RSDP is
accepted on its signature alone. The RSDP is found by scanning low memory for the
eight-byte string `RSD PTR `, which is why the module header calls this checksum
"the whole defence": without it, any sixteen bytes that happen to spell the
signature hand the kernel a physical address it will follow into somebody's data.

Note that the revision-2 path still checks its own extended checksum, so a
firmware-shaped RSDP still parses. That is what makes this defect the plausible
one: every hand-written test in the file that corrupts a byte and expects
`BadChecksum` is a revision-2 RSDP, so the only test that would fail is one nobody
wrote.

Expected to fail: `assert!(is_checksum_ok(&bytes[..RSDP_V1_LEN]))`.

diff --git a/crates/machine_discovery/src/acpi.rs b/crates/machine_discovery/src/acpi.rs
--- a/crates/machine_discovery/src/acpi.rs
+++ b/crates/machine_discovery/src/acpi.rs
